  "description": "## Overview\n\nThis simplified synthetic starter explores repeatable deployment units for a SaaS application. It is not deployed or a guarantee of tenant isolation, regional recovery, or capacity. The boundaries show a shared routing owner and two independently operated stamp units.\n\n## Flow\n\nTenant clients reach Front Door and API Management. A tenant-to-stamp map supplies the placement decision used to route requests to the appropriate App Service. Each stamp owns its SQL database; the drawing deliberately includes no cross-stamp replication path.\n\n## Decisions\n\nChoose tenant placement, routing consistency, onboarding, and capacity rules explicitly. Independent units do not provide automatic failover, data replication, or guaranteed security isolation. Moving a tenant requires a deliberate data and routing plan rather than simply changing an arrow.\n\n## Start Editing\n\nRename the stamps, specify placement metadata, and assign an owner to each application and database pair. Document scaling limits, deployment versions, backup and recovery needs, and tenant-move procedures before extending this view. Keep shared routing dependencies visible when assessing failure impact.\n\n## Reference\n\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/deployment-stamp\n\nChecked 2026-10-17. Original simplified adaptation; synthetic and not deployed. Consult the reference for omitted implementation details."
